course: dictionary
lesson: 4260
title: The grater
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A grater is a kitchen tool with lots of small holes, and the holes have sharp edges. You rub food such as cheese, carrot or potato against the holes, and the food comes off in small pieces. To grate means to do this.
explain: «Graters, peelers and the cut edge of a tin lid take skin off fingers very easily.» This sentence is a warning. The holes and edges are sharp, so they can cut your skin if you are careless. The word «easily» tells you it does not take much.
explain: When you meet a new tool word, ask two questions: what is it used for, and is it sharp? Then check the words around it for clues, such as a warning or a safety rule.
fact: A grater is a kitchen tool with many small sharp-edged holes.
fact: «Grate» means to rub food against a grater so it becomes small pieces.
fact: A box grater has four sides, and each side has holes of a different size.
fact: A fine grater or zester makes very tiny bits of food.
fact: When you grate, hold the food firmly, keep your fingers clear of the holes, and stop when the piece gets small.
exercise: What is a grater? | *A kitchen tool with many small sharp-edged holes | A pan with a lid for boiling water | A bowl for mixing cake batter | A cloth for drying plates
exercise: «Graters, peelers and the cut edge of a tin lid take skin off fingers very easily.» What is this sentence warning you about? | Kitchen cloths get dirty quickly | *Your fingers can be cut easily if you are careless | Peelers are too expensive | Tins can be heavy to lift
exercise: What is the safest way to grate cheese? | *Hold the cheese firmly and keep your fingers well clear of the holes | Grate it while you run around the kitchen | Grate it quickly without looking | Use your bare fingers close to the holes so you can push it hard
test: To grate means to... | *rub food against a grater so it becomes small pieces | boil food in a pan until soft | wrap food in foil to keep it warm | cut food with scissors into big chunks
test: Which step keeps you safe when using a grater? | Use the grater with wet, slippery hands | *Stop when the piece of food gets small | Hold the food with the holes pointing at your palm | Stop only when your fingers start to hurt
exam: Which kitchen tool has four sides, each with holes of a different size? | A kettle | A rolling pin | A fork | *A box grater
